<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Līloa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Liloa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Līloa was ruler (aliʻi nui) of the island of Hawaiʻi in the late 15th century, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waipio-valley">Waipiʻo Valley</a>. Hawaiian tradition remembers him as a respected ruler and the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakau">Hākau</a>, his heir, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umi-a-liloa">ʻUmi-a-Līloa</a>, born to a woman of lower rank, whom he acknowledged as his son. On his death the island passed to Hākau, and the brothers' quarrel led to ʻUmi's revolt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liloa.jpg" alt="Līloa" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer; published alongside papers by Thomas M. Spaulding (b. 1882) and Curtis Piʻehu ʻIaukea (1855–1940) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liloa_and_Lonoikamakahiki.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
